1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. d4 Be7 { We are at the starting position of the Rio de Janeiro Variation of the Berlin Defense. The origin of the name is mysterious, since in databases you will find only one game in this line played in the Brazilian city more than 100 years after the variation's birth (Tal-Panno, 1987). In fact, it is one of the oldest variations known in chess and was upheld by such household names as Steinitz, Anderssen, Lasker, Tarrasch, Pilsburry, Reti, Smyslov, Spassky and others. However, in the 21th century, following the trend set by Kasparov-Kramnik 2000 World Championship match it has given way to l'Hermit Variation: 5...Nd6, leading to the famous Berlin Endgame (also called 'Berlin Wall') Thanks to an overwhelming popularity of 5....Nd6 nowadays, it is very probable that your fifth move: 5... Be7 will already come as a surprise to a majority of White players. If you are already a mainstream Berlin player, it can serve as a great alternative line. However, this line is not only an excellent surprise weapon. After analysing it thoroughly, I am confident that it is solid enough to be used as a main weapon even against high-level opposition. } 6. Qe2 { An absolutely main move and the only really challenging one. } Nd6 7. Bxc6 bxc6 8. dxe5 Nb7 { This is the main position of the Rio de Janeiro Variation, which has been established as White's best chance for an advantage. White has numerous tries here and before examining them, it is important to understand the general features of the position. In my view, the key to its understanding lies in realization that Black's light square bishop is the most important piece on the board. While temporarily buried on c8, it has the potential to become a powerful piece, if developed along the b1-h7 or h1-a8 diagonal. If Black deploys it comfortably, in conjunction with putting his knight on an excellent e6 square, his pieces will enjoy remarkable harmony and he will hardly ever be worse. On the contrary, he will acquire great chances of taking over the initiative. If, however, White succeeds in restricting its development, he will be able to develop play against Black's structural weaknesses and force him to passive defence. Having understood this, it is easy to predict both sides' plans. Black will try to play ...d5 with further ...Bf5 or ...Bb7 and .. .c5 on the cards. White's main idea will be to deny his opponent this possibility and he has 3 main ways to do so: early Re1, intending exd6 after .. .d5, forcing an undesirable ...Bxd6 due to an attack on the e7 bishop; early Nd4, putting c6 under attack; or early c4, intending to take control of the d5 square with Nc3 and Rd1. However, I believe Black has enough resources to meet any of these plans and achieve a fair share of chances. Enough of this lengthy introduction, let's study concrete variations! } 9. Nc3 { The most popular move and the starting point of our chapter covering the main line of Rio de Janeiro Variation. } O-O 10. Re1 { White's most popular way of playing. He intends to meet ... d5 with exd6!, making the desirable ... cxd6 recapture impossible because of the e7 bishop being under attack. 10.Nd4 transposes to 9.Nd4. } Nc5 { Black starts by maneuvering the knight to an optimal position. } 11. Be3 { -KEY- } Ne6 12. Rad1 { A straightforward move, which makes Black's road to equality pretty straightforward, too. This move was considered back in 2003 in the first volume of Khalifman's great series 'Opening for White According to Anand'. } d5 13. exd6 cxd6 14. Nd4 { White has to open the game before Black consolidates and starts making use of his two bishops. } Nxd4 15. Bxd4 Re8 16. Qf3 { White has a temporary initiative but he is unable to extricate anything substantial from it. } Bd7 17. Qg3 { We are still following Khalifman's recommendation. } Bf8 18. Ne4 Re6 $1 { and Black has no problems whatsoever. } 19. c4 Rg6 ( { A new move. Khalifman considered only } 19... d5 { which is not bad but it's really unnecessary to go for the isolated pawn. } ) 
20. Qf4 Be6 { with equality. } 
