 contrary, he will acquire great chances of taking over the initiative. If, however, White succeeds in restricting its development, he will be able to develop play against Black's structural weaknesses and force him to passive defence. Having understood this, it is easy to predict both sides' plans. Black will try to play ...d5 with further ...Bf5 or ...Bb7 and .. .c5 on the cards. White's main idea will be to deny his opponent this possibility and he has 3 main ways to do so: early Re1, intending exd6 after .. .d5, forcing an undesirable ...Bxd6 due to an attack on the e7 bishop; early Nd4, putting c6 under attack; or early c4, intending to take control of the d5 square with Nc3 and Rd1. However, I believe Black has enough resources to meet any of these plans and achieve a fair share of chances. Enough of this lengthy introduction, let's study concrete variations! } 9. Nc3 { The most popular move and the starting point of our chapter covering the main line of Rio de Janeiro Variation. } O-O 10. Re1 { White's most popular way of playing. He intends to meet ... d5 with exd6!, making the desirable ... cxd6 recapture impossible because of the e7 bishop being under attack. 10.Nd4 transposes to 9.Nd4. } Nc5 { Black starts by maneuvering the knight to an optimal position. } 11. Be3 Ne6 12. Nd4 { This is the main move. It's a very direct approach. White again stops the ...d5 break and a certain ingenuity is required from Black. } Rb8 $1 { A key move. The idea is quite common in the Open Games, e.g. the Scotch. White will find it hard to meet the attack on the b2 pawn without making concessions. } 13. Nb3 { -KEY- } f6 $5 { A promising pawn sacrifice. } 14. Bxa7 Rb4 $1 { The rook swing along the 4th rank is the crux of Black's idea. It is worth noting that White's passed 'a' pawn is not as dangerous as it looks at first sight, because even if it advances all the way to a7, it can be easily blocked by Black's light-squared bishop. } 15. exf6 Bxf6 { This exchange gives Black the option to take on c3. } 16. f3 d6 { Thinking about ...c5. } ( { Black could also play } 16... c5 { but } 17. Rad1 d6 18. Bxc5 { is very messy. } ) 
17. a3 Rf4 18. Be3 Rf5 $13 { Black has dangerous play on the kingside, with ...Be5 and ...Qh4 coming. } 
